          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for chronic insomnia?",
          "answer": "Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is described as first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in adults, ahead of medication. This comes from the guidance document \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\" (sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance). The same document says its effects tend to last after treatment ends, while medication effects generally do not, and it avoids the risks of long-term sleep medicines.\n\nThe program is usually delivered over about four to eight sessions and includes components such as stimulus control (using the bed only for sleep) and sleep restriction (limiting time in bed toward actual sleep time). The same guidance says medication is second-line and is best used for a limited time.\n\nThe \"Insomnia\" general reference (condition-insomnia) also says sleep hygiene habits alone are not effective treatment for chronic insomnia. The retrieved passages did not give further detail on the evidence behind these points, so I can't say more about it here.\n\nA clinician can help decide whether CBT-I or other options fit a particular situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
